# Schema System

Schemas are authoritative contracts, not hints inferred from data. The graph
facade, import pipeline, editor, and agent tools all preserve the loaded object
types, property definitions, required fields, edge types, and allowed endpoint
pairs.

## Implementation boundaries

- `crates/u-forge-core/src/schema/definition.rs` owns
  `SchemaDefinition`, object/property/edge schemas, and validation types.
- `crates/u-forge-core/src/schema/manager.rs` persists schemas and caches them
  in a `parking_lot::RwLock<HashMap<...>>`.
- `crates/u-forge-core/src/schema/ingestion.rs` reads `.schema.json` files,
  strips the external `add_` action prefix, and derives edge schemas from
  properties that declare `relationship` metadata.
- `KnowledgeGraph::merged_schema_definition()` is the structured union used by
  the agent. Request-bounded prompt selection happens in
  `u-forge-agent::budget`, not in `SchemaManager`.

`SchemaManager::save_schema` is synchronous. Some higher-level validation and
registration methods remain async because they load persisted schema state;
do not make callers await the synchronous save path.

## Schema file contract

Files in `defaults/schemas/` use the MCP-shaped external name and per-property
required flag:

```json
{
  "name": "add_npc",
  "description": "Add an NPC",
  "properties": {
    "name": { "type": "string", "required": true },
    "role": {
      "type": "string",
      "required": true,
      "validation": { "allowed_values": ["merchant", "guard"] }
    },
    "currentLocation": {
      "type": "string",
      "relationship": {
        "edgeType": "located_in",
        "targetType": "location"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

The internal object type is `npc`. Relationship metadata makes the owning
object type an allowed source and `targetType` an allowed target; no unrelated
“common edge” registry is added implicitly.

Supported property kinds are `string`, `text`, `number`, `boolean`, `array`,
`object`, `reference`, and `enum`. Validation rules include length/range,
regex, and allowed-value constraints.

## Validation boundaries

`SchemaManager::validate_and_coerce_properties` reports missing required
properties, unknown properties, type mismatches, and invalid enum values while
coercing only compatible primitive representations. Callers must consume the
issues; invoking the helper and discarding them defeats the schema boundary.

When one or more schemas are persisted, the JSONL importer is stricter than the
general warning-oriented validation API:

- unknown object/edge types, missing required properties, and invalid endpoint
  pairs skip the record;
- undeclared properties are dropped;
- coercible values are normalized;
- skipped records and dropped fields are counted and written to the import
  diagnostics sidecar.

Never derive a schema from the records being imported, register an observed
unknown type, preserve undeclared fields, or choose an endpoint outside the
declared source/target sets. Accepted shape changes require an explicit schema
edit and schema import.

`DataIngestion` retains a legacy schema-less compatibility mode when its graph
contains no persisted schema: it maps known node kinds through `ObjectBuilder`
and stores open properties. The desktop UI prevents entry into this mode by
disabling data import until a non-default schema is present. Core callers that
need authoritative validation must also persist a schema before invoking
`import_data_only`; the low-level compatibility behavior is not a strict import.

## Persistence and UI import

The desktop app does not auto-import `defaults/schemas` on startup. **File →
Import Schema…** loads the selected directory into the `imported_schemas`
namespace. **File → Import Data…** is enabled only when persisted schemas are
present. Core callers use `SchemaIngestion::load_schemas_from_directory`
followed by synchronous `SchemaManager::save_schema`.

The included schema types are: `npc`, `player_character`, `location`,
`faction`, `quest`, `artifact`, `currency`, `inventory`, `skills`, `temporal`,
`setting_reference`, `system_reference`, and `transportation`.

## Naming constraint

The `add_` prefix is an external MCP action convention retained in schema file
names. Internal graph types never include it. Keep prefix stripping at the
ingestion boundary rather than leaking action names into stored object types.
